Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 08:36:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10885/11171] Correct the doc/YAML hypothesis: the constant is
 the document, not the YAML

Milestone 607 was the test the previous entry named, and it refutes that
entry's own narrowing. 607 declares 14 tasks in the planner document and queued
7 in the YAML. Every measured divergence having a 4-task YAML is therefore
false, and the fixed-truncation-point reading it supported goes with it.

What the three milestones actually show is that the document is the stable
quantity: 14 planned in both divergent cases, 12 in the one that came out
clean, with the YAML holding 4 and then 7. That points at a budget a 14-task
plan exceeds and a 12-task plan fits, with the YAML keeping the prefix that
fit. Recorded explicitly as n=3 and not established.

The timeout link is weakened in the same observation. The previous entry asked
whether a retried planner truncates, since 606's planner had timed out first.
607 diverged with no preceding planner failure at all, so a timeout is not
necessary for divergence.

Seven declared tasks, exp6934 through exp6940 including the v607 capstone, have
no YAML entry and will not run.

Replaces the two experiments the previous entry proposed with a cheaper and
more decisive one: sweep document count against YAML count across every
milestone in the record, rather than only those a contract experiment happened
to measure. That either establishes the budget reading or kills it.

